Subject: [PATCH 0004] extensionManagement: signature verification for a non-Microsoft gallery
Refs: ADR-0009, ADR-0010
Status: UNWRITTEN — header and intent only, no diff hunks. See below.

STATUS
======

This file contains no diff. It is disabled by its `.patch.no` suffix.

This patch also cannot be written correctly from documentation alone: the exact
shape depends on how the current upstream release structures
ExtensionSignatureVerificationService and what it does when the verifier module is
absent, and that has changed across releases.

INTENT
======

Make extension installation work against a gallery whose extensions are not signed
by Microsoft, without silently disabling integrity checking and without pretending
a check happened that did not.

THE PROBLEM
===========

Upstream verifies extension packages with `@vscode/vsce-sign`, a proprietary
Microsoft-published binary, against signatures produced by the Microsoft
Marketplace's signing infrastructure.

ADR-0009 points the gallery at a self-hosted Open VSX instance, because the
Marketplace Terms of Use prohibit fork access. Open VSX does not produce
Microsoft's signatures. So every install attempt reaches a verification step that
cannot succeed — not because the extension is bad, but because the signature it is
being checked against does not exist.

This is the adjustment every Code-OSS fork has to make. It is listed in ADR-0010's
minimum in-tree patch set as "upstream hardcodes an assumption forks break".

WHAT NOT TO DO
==============

The shortest fix is to make the verification function return "verified"
unconditionally. Do not do that.

It produces a build that reports every extension as signature-verified, including
one that has been tampered with in transit. A user reading that UI has been
actively misled, which is worse than a build that says nothing. The same reasoning
appears in this project's applier rules about ValidationResult.validator: a field
that claims evidence must not be widened to cover evidence that was never
gathered.

WHAT TO DO INSTEAD
==================

Make "this gallery does not sign extensions" a distinct, honest state:

  * Verification is skipped, not faked. The install proceeds and the extension is
    reported as unsigned.
  * The gallery says whether it signs. A product.json flag on extensionsGallery is
    the natural place, so the behaviour follows the configured registry rather than
    a compile-time constant.
  * Transport integrity still holds. Extensions are fetched over TLS from a host
    we operate, and Open VSX publishes a SHA-256 per version. Checking the digest
    is strictly better than skipping verification and is available without
    Microsoft's signing chain.

Point 3 is the part worth doing properly, and it is why this patch is medium risk
rather than low: it is not a deletion, it is a small replacement.

FILES
=====

  src/vs/platform/extensionManagement/node/extensionSignatureVerificationService.ts
  src/vs/platform/extensionManagement/common/extensionManagement.ts   (the enum/result type)
  src/vs/base/common/product.ts                                       (the gallery flag; see 0006)

HOW TO WRITE IT
===============

  1. Read the current service. Establish what it does when vsce-sign is missing
     entirely — a recent upstream may already have a "not available" path, in which
     case this patch is much smaller than the above suggests.
  2. Add the gallery flag to product.json via apps/ide/branding/product.delta.jq
     and to the type via patch 0006.
  3. Skip verification when the flag says the gallery is unsigned; verify the
     published digest instead.
  4. Confirm the extensions view reports the extension as unsigned. If the UI
     claims verification, the patch is wrong even if installation works.
  5. git -C apps/ide/vscode diff adze/pristine \
       -- src/vs/platform/extensionManagement \
       > apps/ide/patches/0004-extension-signature-verification.patch

Step 4 is the acceptance criterion. Installation working is not.
